      <header class="page-header">
        <h1>How to Towel Hang: Complete Guide</h1>
        <p class="subtitle">A towel over a pull-up bar makes the hang harder on the fingers. No extra equipment needed.</p>
      </header>
    </div>
    <article class="article">
      <div class="article-meta">
        <a href="/about/" class="author-link">
          <span class="author-avatar">DH</span>
          <span>DeadHangs Team</span>
        </a>
        <span class="meta-sep">·</span>
        <span class="updated-date">Updated October 30, 2026</span>
        
      </div><div class="container">
      <h2>What Is a Towel Hang</h2>
      <p>A towel hang is a dead hang with a towel draped over the bar, gripping the towel ends instead of the bar. Hang with straight arms and let your bodyweight pull down through the fabric. Your fingers grip soft cloth instead of rigid steel.</p>
      <p>The towel makes the support grip harder because the fingers cannot lock around a rigid bar. The fabric shifts under load, so you adjust your hold as you hang.</p>
      <p>Climbers and grappling athletes commonly use towel or gi hangs. A pull-up bar offers a uniform cylinder, and a towel offers a surface that moves.</p>

      <h2>Why Towel Hangs Are Harder</h2>
      <p>A folded towel is thicker and softer than a bar, so the fingers cannot wrap fully. Many people hold for less time than on a bar.</p>
      <p>The surface is unstable. Fabric shifts, bunches and rotates under load, which calls for constant small adjustments in your grip.</p>
      <p>A bar holds your wrist at a fixed angle, and a towel lets the wrist move more. Your wrist stabilizers have more to do.</p>
      <p>There is no published ratio between towel hang time and bar hang time. Expect a shorter hold than on a bar and track the two separately.</p>

      <h2>How to Set Up a Towel Hang</h2>
      <ol>
        <li><strong>Choose your towel.</strong> A standard gym towel (about 16 x 28 inches) or bath towel works. Avoid thin hand towels because they bunch tightly and cut into the fingers.</li>
        <li><strong>Fold lengthwise.</strong> Fold the towel in half or thirds along its length. This makes a grip surface about 3 to 4 inches wide. Wider folds are harder and narrower folds are easier.</li>
        <li><strong>Drape over the bar.</strong> Center the towel over the pull-up bar so both sides hang evenly, with about 12 to 18 inches hanging below the bar on each side.</li>
        <li><strong>Grip at shoulder width.</strong> Grab one side of the towel in each hand. Position your hands at shoulder width. Squeeze the fabric firmly before stepping off.</li>
        <li><strong>Test with partial weight.</strong> Bend your knees and let the towel take some bodyweight. Confirm the towel does not slide on the bar before committing full weight. Hang with your feet close to the floor over a mat in case the towel slips.</li>
        <li><strong>Hang.</strong> Step off fully. Keep your arms straight and breathe slowly. Stop one or two seconds before your grip gives out and step down under control. Do not hang to failure or drop.</li>
      </ol>

      <h2>Towel Hang Variations</h2>
      <h3>Single Towel (Both Hands)</h3>
      <p>Drape one towel over the bar and grip both hanging ends. Your hands sit close together. This is the standard setup and the best starting point for towel hang training.</p>

      <h3>Double Towel (One Per Hand)</h3>
      <p>Drape two separate towels over the bar at shoulder width. Grip one towel per hand. This allows a wider grip and lets you adjust towel thickness independently for each hand.</p>

      <h3>Thick Towel</h3>
      <p>Use a beach towel or fold a bath towel to triple thickness. A thicker towel is harder to wrap. Thick towel hangs are the hardest variation and suit people who have mastered standard towel hangs.</p>

      <h3>Thin Towel</h3>
      <p>A thin hand towel or tea towel makes a narrower grip surface. Your fingers wrap further around the fabric, which gives a better hold. Thin towels suit beginners moving from bar hangs to towel hangs for the first time.</p>

      <h2>Programming Towel Hangs</h2>
      <p>Treat towel hangs as supplemental grip work rather than a replacement for standard dead hangs. Your primary hang training should still use a bar. Towel hangs add variety.</p>

      <div class="callout">
        <h3>Towel Hang Protocol</h3>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>Sets:</strong> 3 sets per session</li>
          <li><strong>Time:</strong> 10 to 20 seconds per set, stopping a second or two before your grip gives out</li>
          <li><strong>Rest:</strong> 90 seconds between sets</li>
          <li><strong>Frequency:</strong> 1 or 2 sessions a week, with at least one rest day between hard sessions</li>
          <li><strong>Placement:</strong> After main dead hang work</li>
        </ul>
        <p>The towel hang protocol is common training practice, not a tested prescription.</p>
      </div>

      <p>Add towel hangs at the end of your grip session. Your forearms will already be tired from bar hangs, so keep the sets short and stop before your grip fails.</p>
      <p>Track your towel hang times separately from bar hang times, because the two are different tests. Add about 5 seconds to your target hold once you complete every set cleanly, roughly once a week. For the wider picture on hand strength, see the <a href="/grip-strength/">grip strength guide</a>.</p>

      <h2>Progression</h2>
      <p>Follow this sequence from easiest to hardest. Move up when you can hold the target time for two sessions in a row. The target times are practical targets, not norms.</p>

      <p class="table-cue">Scroll the table horizontally on smaller screens.</p><div class="table-scroll" role="region" aria-label="Scrollable comparison table" tabindex="0"><table class="data-table">
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th scope="col">Level</th>
            <th scope="col">Setup</th>
            <th scope="col">Target Time</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr>
            <td><strong>1. Thin towel</strong></td>
            <td>Thin towel, double (one per hand)</td>
            <td>15 to 20 seconds</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><strong>2. Standard towel</strong></td>
            <td>Gym towel, double (one per hand)</td>
            <td>15 to 20 seconds</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><strong>3. Single towel</strong></td>
            <td>One gym towel, both hands on same towel</td>
            <td>12 to 15 seconds</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><strong>4. Thick towel</strong></td>
            <td>Beach towel or triple-folded bath towel</td>
            <td>10 to 15 seconds</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><strong>5. Single towel one-arm</strong></td>
            <td>One towel, one hand (advanced)</td>
            <td>5 to 10 seconds</td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table></div>

      <p>The one-arm towel hang is an advanced exercise. Attempt it only after you can hold the level 4 target time and meet the prerequisite in the <a href="/one-arm-deadhang/">one-arm dead hang guide</a>.</p>

      <h2>Who Should Use Towel Hangs</h2>
      <h3>Climbers</h3>
      <p>Rock and indoor climbing demand grip on irregular surfaces that never feel like a pull-up bar. Climbers add towel hangs to vary grip. Keep it to 1 or 2 sessions per week as in the protocol above.</p>

      <h3>Martial Artists</h3>
      <p>Judo, BJJ and wrestling involve gripping fabric such as a gi. A towel is a soft, thick object, similar in some ways to a gi.</p>

      <h3>Anyone Who Wants Variety</h3>
      <p>A towel adds difficulty without extra equipment when bar hangs start to feel easy. No study has tested whether towel hangs break a plateau.</p>
      <p>Thick <a href="/grip-attachments/">fat grip attachments</a> are another way to add difficulty on a bar.</p>

      <h2>Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
      <div class="faq">
        <h3>Why are towel hangs harder than regular dead hangs?</h3>
        <p>Towel hangs usually end sooner than bar hangs because the surface is thicker and shifts under load. A thick towel stops your fingers from wrapping fully, and the fabric moves under your hands, so you adjust your grip as you hang.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="faq">
        <h3>What kind of towel should I use for towel hangs?</h3>
        <p>Use a standard gym towel or bath towel. Thicker towels are harder. Fold the towel lengthwise to make a manageable grip width. The towel must be strong enough to support your bodyweight without tearing. Test with partial weight before committing, and hang over a mat with your feet close to the floor.</p>
      </div>

